# The man pages for these are built with sphinx, which pulls a python
# documentation toolchain and, through it, a package whose USE constraints
# cannot be satisfied here. A rescue image wants the tools, not the manuals.
sys-fs/btrfs-progs -man
sys-fs/xfsprogs -libedit
sys-apps/nvme-cli -json

# Without this lvm2 builds only device-mapper, so a stick meant for repairing
# machines would have no way to bring up a volume group.
sys-fs/lvm2 lvm

# The manuals are built from docbook, which drags in a documentation
# toolchain for a rescue image that has no use for it.
sys-apps/dbus -doc
sys-apps/dbus systemd

# Portage's systemd rather than the hand built one. sysv-utils makes systemd
# provide halt, reboot, shutdown and poweroff itself; without it the ebuild
# pulls sysvinit back in to supply them. The rest is kept close to what the
# hand built configuration had, so this is the same systemd under new
# management rather than a larger one.
sys-apps/systemd sysv-utils kmod -acl -dns-over-tls -gcrypt -kernel-install
# pam is already how the rest of the image authenticates: util-linux was
# built with it and pambase is installed, so systemd uses it too rather
# than being the one thing that does not.
sys-apps/systemd pam -libarchive -lz4 -openssl -pcre -resolvconf -seccomp -zstd
